    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In a DC circuit, a capacitor initially charges up and then acts as an open circuit, effectively blocking the steady current flow. Conversely, in an AC circuit, the voltage continuously changes, allowing the capacitor to store and release charge, thus letting current pass through it. The resistance a capacitor offers to AC is called capacitive reactance ($\\text{X}_\\text{C}$), and it is calculated using the formula $\\text{X}_\\text{C} = \\frac{1}{2\\pi fC}$, where $f$ is the frequency and $C$ is the capacitance.",
